questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"valence-ox-states","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-pe-232","chapter_id":"chemistry-classification-of-elements-and-periodicity-in-properties","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Carbon is tetravalent in the great majority of its stable compounds because it has","options":["two valence electrons","four valence electrons","six valence electrons","no valence electrons"],"answer_index":1,"explanation":"The 2s² 2p² set provides four electrons for four covalent bonds (as in CH₄).","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"valence-ox-states","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-pe-233","chapter_id":"chemistry-classification-of-elements-and-periodicity-in-properties","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Transition metals are noted for showing","options":["only the oxidation state +1","variable oxidation states","no cations at all","a unique oxidation state of −4"],"answer_index":1,"explanation":"Both ns and (n−1)d electrons can take part in bonding, so several OS are common.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"valence-ox-states","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-pe-234","chapter_id":"chemistry-classification-of-elements-and-periodicity-in-properties","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"In most of its compounds oxygen exhibits the oxidation state","options":["+2","−2","+6","0 only"],"answer_index":1,"explanation":"Oxygen is highly electronegative and usually completes its octet by gaining or sharing two electrons.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"valence-ox-states","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-pe-235","chapter_id":"chemistry-classification-of-elements-and-periodicity-in-properties","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Nitrogen can show oxidation states ranging from −3 in ammonia to +5 in","options":["nitrogen gas","nitric acid","hydrazine","the nitride ion"],"answer_index":1,"explanation":"HNO₃ and nitrates use all five valence electrons of N; NH₃ uses the −3 state.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"medium","concept_id":"valence-ox-states","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false}]$json$::jsonb) as x(
